# Soma's plan, in John Carmack's .plan format: one item per line, under the
# day it was written. An unmarked line is open; the leader marks an item
# "+ " when it judges it done (or "* " the day it was written), "- " if
# dropped; bids make progress on it, whole or in parts. Marked lines
# are pruned once they've landed, so the file holds what's left: `git log -p
# .plan` has the rest. The order is the priority, a guide: the leader may
# take another item when it's the better next step. An item is a pointer: its
# details are in the files it names. Pantheon's docs/PLAN.md explains how its
# market drafts tasks from this file.

2026-11-10
soma build's module cache crosses checkouts: compiled modules are cached by content under ~/.cache/soma/HASH (XDG_CACHE_HOME isn't honored for them), and a .definition's .vo records the absolute path of the .ml beside it, so another checkout with the same .definition compiles the first checkout's .ml: a bidder testing a shell.ml change in a scratch checkout on its node's machine builds the node's shell.ml instead (Pantheon at 6161fc9 exported to a new directory compiled /Users/yev/Development/pantheon/shell.ml). Resolve the .ml beside the .definition being built, or key the cache by it, and honor XDG_CACHE_HOME; judged by a misc test that builds two copies of a project differing only in the .ml and checks each program prints its own (plugins/soma_ocaml/soma_ocaml.ml bind_module, toplevel/somaOcaml.ml)
+ Sandbox, checkable without booting: Soma's CI is Linux with no solo5 or MirageOS switch, so a change to Sandbox can't be checked there today. `soma build --no-image` (or no $SOMA_MIRAGE_SWITCH) checks a Sandbox program, extracts it and generates its MirageOS project, then type-checks prog.ml and soma_rt.ml against stand-ins for the Mirage modules it uses, stopping short of the image. Floor: a test-suite/misc/soma_*.sh test runs it on examples/example_files/mirage_* and on a deliberately ill-typed soma_rt change; dev/mirage-check.sh still passes on a Mac with the hvf switch (toplevel/somaMirage.ml, GRADUAL.md "Sandbox")
Sandbox, links that stay open: the node keeps a TCP connection to each peer and sends and receives framed messages on it. Add connections to io: listen on a port with a handler per connection, connect, send, receive (a line or a length-prefixed frame), close, with Refused / TimedOut / Closed results like fetch's. Floor: Sandbox.v and soma_rt in toplevel/somaMirage.ml; an example of two images exchanging messages over one connection, in dev/mirage-check.sh; a misc test through --no-image (theories/Soma/Sandbox.v, toplevel/somaMirage.ml; Pantheon's docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Two directions, and the unikernel")
Sandbox, a millisecond clock: a monotonic clock in milliseconds and a sleep in milliseconds (the node's block timer is 1000 ms and its timeouts are monotonic, Pantheon's D-2). Floor: as the links item: Sandbox.v, soma_rt, an example in dev/mirage-check.sh, a misc test (theories/Soma/Sandbox.v, toplevel/somaMirage.ml)
Sandbox, storage: the node keeps its key, its block log and its consensus state across restarts. Add reading, writing and appending named files on the image's block device (solo5's block target, mirage-kv or a log of its own), with a result for a missing file. Floor: an example that counts its boots on a disk image, in dev/mirage-check.sh, and a misc test through --no-image (theories/Soma/Sandbox.v, toplevel/somaMirage.ml)
Sandbox, SHA-256 and RSA: the node hashes blocks and signs and verifies transactions. Add them as Sandbox functions (mirage-crypto), giving byte for byte what Pantheon's binary gives (its shell.ml sha256, sign and verify, with its key format), checked on Pantheon's own test vectors. Floor: a misc test with known answers through --no-image, and an image in dev/mirage-check.sh that verifies a signature a Pantheon binary made (theories/Soma/Sandbox.v, toplevel/somaMirage.ml)
Sandbox, services: git, the model and CI run as processes beside the binary and as services the unikernel asks over the network. fetch has a 10-second limit and no request framing; give it a timeout argument (a model call takes minutes) and HTTP helpers for a POST with a body and a status, so a node can call a git, model or CI service over HTTP. Floor: an example that POSTs to a server image and reads its status and body, in dev/mirage-check.sh; a misc test through --no-image (theories/Soma/Sandbox.v, toplevel/somaMirage.ml)
